# Read the armour check penalty of every shield in the game off the character
# dump, once each, on the same character. Bead inc-rsps.
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. Armour::PenaltyVal (src/Item.cpp:2231) used to price
# a shield from a size comparison alone and never read the item's own
# Penalty:, so no shield could differ from another of its size and the buckler
# cost nothing at all. The figures the game now owes a Medium character are
# the ones lib/weapons.irh authors: buckler -1, small shield -2, kite shield
# -4, tower shield -10.
#
# THE ORACLE is the character dump written by the sheet's [W] command
# (src/Managers.cpp:2503, src/Sheet.cpp:1389), which lands in the run's own
# logs/ directory. Its Skill Ratings block prints the armour term of every
# skill separately -- "Balance -7 (0 ranks, +3 DEX, -10 armour)" -- and its
# Movement Rate line prints the shield factor separately from encumbrance.
# Two independent readings of the same number, and neither is a screen scrape.
# tools/check_shield_penalty.sh does the arithmetic.
#
# WHY A PALADIN. He is Medium, so the authored figure is what he pays with no
# size step applied, and he is shield-proficient through his Grants block, so
# no non-proficiency penalty is mixed into the reading. He also arrives
# wearing a kite shield on a shoulder, which is the first subject.
#
# THE BASELINE ("none") HAS NO Balance LINE AT ALL, and that is not a fault.
# The sheet lists a skill only once something moves it; Balance at 0 ranks
# with nothing but its DEX modifier is left out. Its absence is the reading:
# no shield is in the hand, so no armour term exists. The Movement Rate line
# carries the baseline that can be compared.
#
# Menu letters move when a list changes. Run at the seed the check supplies
# and no other.

@include chargen-paladin.keys

@until "Afoot" ESC

# --- BASELINE: the kite shield is on a shoulder, so nothing is readied ------
d
W
n o n e
ENTER
ESC

# --- KITE SHIELD: off the shoulder and into the hand ------------------------
# SPACE lifts the selected slot's item into the air; SPACE on the destination
# slot puts it down there.
i
f
SPACE
a
SPACE
@dump:kite-in-hand
ESC
d
W
k i t e
ENTER
ESC

# --- Wizard mode, for the three shields he does not own ---------------------
w
@expect "Enter wizard mode"
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
DOWN
SPACE           # Auto-Identify Items, so no reading is of an unknown item
ESC

# Shields are the seventeenth entry of ItemGlyphs (src/Tables.cpp:320-337),
# hence RIGHT*16. The list is Tower, Kite, Small, Buckler, Buckler, and it
# opens on the first row, so DOWN counts from Tower. Both bucklers are drawn
# as "Buckler" and both are authored -1, so either row answers.
#
# Placing a new shield in the Ready Hand pushes the old one into the air, and
# the ESC that closes the screen drops it on the floor. That is deliberate:
# each reading must have exactly one shield in hand.

# --- SMALL SHIELD -----------------------------------------------------------
w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*16
DOWN DOWN
ENTER
a
SPACE
@dump:small-in-hand
ESC
d
W
s m a l l
ENTER
ESC

# --- BUCKLER ----------------------------------------------------------------
w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*16
DOWN DOWN DOWN
ENTER
a
SPACE
@dump:buckler-in-hand
ESC
d
W
b u c k
ENTER
ESC

# --- TOWER SHIELD -----------------------------------------------------------
# It needs both hands, so the long sword leaves the Weapon Hand first and is
# dropped. Without this the game refuses the grip and the reading would be of
# an empty hand.
i
b
SPACE
ESC
w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*16
ENTER
a
SPACE
@dump:tower-in-hand
ESC
d
W
t o w e r
ENTER
ESC
@quit
